# %{msil_netfx_skip} and msil-run.py --skip-netfx (plan 5.17.2, 5.17.5;
# M24). The placeholder is empty by default and sits in the .NET Framework
# run of every composite; a test of a construct that the Framework lacks
# (the attribute of --uco-thunks) sets REDEFINE: %{msil_netfx_skip} =
# --skip-netfx '<reason>', and msil-run.py --netfx --skip-netfx REASON then
# prints 'msil-run: netfx run skipped: REASON' and exits 0 without running.
# (1) msil-run.py itself, on Inputs/exit-42.il assembled as an EXE: the skip
# passes even with a wrong expected file and writes nothing to --out; the
# same run without the skip runs and compares; --skip-netfx without --netfx,
# or with an empty reason, is a usage error (status 2).
# (2) The composite, in a nested lit with -a of prog/Inputs/nested-netfx
# (msil-prog.py with the stand-ins of prog/Inputs/prog-cc.py, whose MSIL
# program exits with one more on the .NET Framework: prog-netfx): skip.c
# passes only through its skip, and its Framework step carries the reason and
# prints the skip line; noskip.c, the same test without the skip, fails at
# its Framework run; skip-main-wrong.c fails at its main run, which the skip
# does not cover. Only under the Windows triple, where %msil_exec runs
# locally: the WSL runner has no --netfx run.
# REQUIRES: system-windows, msil-triple-windows, msil-netfx

# RUN: rm -rf %t.d && mkdir -p %t.d
# RUN: %ilasm -exe -output=%t.d/exit-42.exe %S/Inputs/exit-42.il
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t.d/wrong.expected "[exit 41]"

